------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Exception: MIT-licensed client crates
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following crates are licensed under the MIT License (see each crate's
LICENSE file), not the AGPL:

  - native/mjolnir_client     — the `mj` / `mjolnir` CLI
  - native/mjolnir_api        — HTTP client + OIDC token store (also used by
                                external apps such as Papyrus)
  - native/mjolnir_protocol   — vsock / PTY wire protocol

These exist so consumers, other apps, and package managers can ship a
Mjolnir client without AGPL obligations. They do not include the
orchestrator, guest agent, gateway, or blob door.

The MIT crates do not depend on AGPL crates. AGPL crates may depend on the
MIT crates (the usual direction). That cut is load-bearing: if protocol or
api were AGPL, the CLI could not be MIT.

The rest of this repository — Elixir orchestrator (`lib/`), guest agent,
gateway, blob door, bootstrap/install scripts, systemd units, Forge — is
AGPL-3.0-or-later.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Hardware, mini-hosters, and World Tree LLC
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

AGPL covers the software. It does not restrict selling hardware. World Tree
LLC sells mesh nodes that provide an Iroh-addressed base layer. Anyone may
run this software on a mini-PC they own (and, when ARM lands, a Raspberry
Pi). Running unmodified AGPL software as a mini-hoster does not create extra
source-sharing duties beyond pointing users at this repository. Modified
network services must comply with AGPL section 13.

A separate commercial license for the **host** is available from the
Foundation — see COMMERCIAL-LICENSE.md. It is not required to sell
boxes that run unmodified Mjolnir, with this repository as the source
offer. The MIT CLI never needs it.

Contributions are accepted under the Contributor License Agreement in
CLA.md, which is what makes the split- and dual-license model possible.

